/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199427 #edb/ref 1061 67913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199427 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "01644308-2657-5bfc-bdad-7c6353f7293a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199427 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199428 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199428 #edb/ref 1061 67916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199428 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "82de79d7-d2f1-5a78-845b-a3a4a47ee4c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199428 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186199552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199429 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186199430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199429 #edb/ref 1061 67917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199429 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ed9a110-74bf-5cdf-8e45-8f23b994a145" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199429 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199430 #edb/ref 1061 67920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199430 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "03fd2408-ced4-5196-8d53-1d732ef57031" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199430 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186198466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199552 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also define sets using so-called *conditional definitions* of the form\n\n$$\n\\{ {\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}} \\: : \\: {\\color{blue}\\textrm{conditions}} \\}\n$$\n\nwhere our set contains only those elements of the ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}}$ that satisfy the given ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{conditions}}$ (or restrictions).\n\nFor example, the set $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = \\{n\\in \\mathbb Z \\::\\: -2\\leq n\\leq 2\\}\n$$\n\nis defined conditionally and contains all integers between $-2$ and $2$ inclusive:\n\n$$\nA = \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\n$$\n\nIt's often helpful to describe sets using conditional definitions. For example, let's rewrite the following set using a conditional definition.\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{50,51,52,53,\\ldots\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe set $S$ contains all natural numbers greater than or equal to $50,$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb N \\::\\: n\\geq 50\\}\n$$\n\nThe conditional definition of a set is not unique. We can write down an equivalent definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb N \\::\\: n\\gt 49\\}\n$$\n\nUnder this definition, $S$ contains all natural numbers greater than $49,$ *excluding* $49.$ \n\nAlthough $\\mathbb N$ is the most natural base set in this instance, we can also define $S$ using the set of integers $\\mathbb Z$ as the base set. For example,\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb Z \\::\\: n\\geq 50\\}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199552 #edb/ref 1065 8228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199552 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a5b5e889-bdb2-5707-90ab-be875064cc73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199552 #edb/ref 1067 "Conditional Definitions of Sets" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199577 #edb/ref 1064 "As previously seen, we can define sets using so-called *constructive definitions* of the form\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ {\\color{blue}\\textrm{rule}} \\: : \\: {\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}} \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere the ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{rule}}$ is an expression (or formula) that depends on a variable that *constructs* elements of our set when the variable runs over some ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}}.$ \n\nFor example, the set $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ 2n+1\\, : \\, n\\in\\mathbb N_0 \\big \\}\n$$\n\nis defined constructively and contains all positive odd numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n+1\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}} \\\\  & ={2(0)+1,\\,2(1)+1,\\,2(2)+1,\\,2(3)+1,\\,…} \\\\  & ={1,3,5,7,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSometimes, it's helpful to write a set using a constructive definition. For example, how might we express the following set constructively?\n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ 4, 7, 10, 13, \\ldots \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n4,\\quad 7,\\quad 10,\\quad 13,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3 \\\\  & =7-4 \\\\  & =10-7 \\\\  & =13-10 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb N$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)\\cdot d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set constructively as follows:\n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ 3n+1 \\: : \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe expression $3n+1$ generates the elements of our set when $n$ runs over $\\mathbb{N}.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- A bar \"$\\mid$\" is just as acceptable as a colon to separate the rule from the base set. So, we could also express our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB=\\big\\{ 3n+1 \\: \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big\\}\n$$\n\n- The constructive definition of a given set is typically not unique. For example, we could also define $B$ as follows:\n\n$$\nB=\\big\\{ 3n+4 \\: :\\: n \\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used $\\mathbb N_0$ as the base set.\n\nThis definition does indeed generate all elements of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3n+4\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}} & ={3(0)+4,\\,3(1)+4,\\,3(2)+4,\\,3(3)+4,\\,…} \\\\  & ={4,\\,7,\\,10,\\,13,\\,…} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199577 #edb/ref 1065 8307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199577 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "57358da7-7aa7-5201-b3bd-e43581b18a41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186199577 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285066 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f70558b-9b80-5074-a897-55d56cf8c03f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285229 #edb/ref 1080 "N" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285229 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285229 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "30c607b5-a5c7-59f2-a7f7-9e387733756b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175921862853