17592186285229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285695 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287548 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287548 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287548 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186285229 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287548 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c4514aa9-a19c-542d-9372-4e689994fcb0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287574 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186285503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287574 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287574 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287574 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186285503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287574 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cfb29df0-619b-574f-9a2b-69bb58650179" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287849 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287849 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183704" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287849 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287849 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${-\\frac{1}{2},3}$ given below.\n\n${-\\frac{1}{2},3}={q\\in Q\\,:\\,4{q}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$q\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$=0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287849 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "257b25c4-c664-5a50-8385-3bced23d988c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287849 #edb/ref 1074 "The given set contains the roots $q\\in Q$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(q).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${q}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${q}_{2}=3,$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=4,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(q) & =a(q-{q}_{1})(q-{q}_{2}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (q-(-\\frac{1}{2}))(q-3) \\\\  & =4(q+\\frac{1}{2})(q-3) \\\\  & =4({q}^{2}-\\frac{5q}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =4{q}^{2}-10q-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n{-\\frac{1}{2},3}={q\\in Q\\,:\\,4{q}^{2}-10q-6=0}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287853 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287853 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287853 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287853 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set ${1\\pm \\text{i}}$ given below.\n\n${1\\pm \\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,2{z}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-1}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$=0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287853 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2b6a6ba-df80-5524-9383-3ef68a2189d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287853 #edb/ref 1074 "The given set contains the roots $z\\in C$ of some quadratic polynomial $p(z).$\n\nSince the quadratic polynomial has roots ${z}_{1}=1-\\text{i}$ and ${z}_{2}=1+\\text{i},$ and the coefficient of the quadratic term is $a=2,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z) & =a(z-{z}_{1})(z-{z}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (z-(1-\\text{i}))(z-(1+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(1-\\text{i})z-(1+\\text{i})z+(1-\\text{i})(1+\\text{i})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(1-\\text{i}+1+\\text{i})z+((1)^{2}-(\\text{i})^{2})) \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({z}^{2}-(2)z+(1+1)) \\\\  & =2{z}^{2}-4z+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as follows:\n\n$$\n{1\\pm \\text{i}}={z\\in C\\,:\\,2{z}^{2}-4z+4=0}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287864 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186287548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287864 #edb/ref 1069 "q-183792" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287864 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287864 #edb/ref 1071 "Describe the set $S={2,1,\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4},...}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n$$\nS={{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287864 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "652769f0-9ddf-5075-bef3-a5f8f9537f1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186287864 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n2,\\,1,\\,\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\frac{1}{4},\\,…\n$$\n\nform an infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in N,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS={4\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186390165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1077 "field-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186390162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390160 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8b58d997-a72e-5be2-a36a-0dcd6a3375be" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390161 #edb/ref 1080 "52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390161 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186390161 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58adad53-8ca3-58fb